From 6372eb0219ac7fb2a6d1240f6f9beea384ea7e27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 06:06:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/20] avcodec/ppc: VP9 horizontal loop filters (4/8-wide +
 mix2) AltiVec (O2)

Add the horizontal (column-edge) VP9 loop filters to vp9dsp_altivec.c,
reusing the existing vertical (row-edge) filter arithmetic via an 8x8
transpose:

  - loop_filter_h_4_8 / loop_filter_h_8_8       (loop_filter_8[0/1][0])
  - loop_filter_h_{44,48,84,88}_16              (loop_filter_mix2[*][*][0])

The 4-wide narrow filter and the 8-wide flat8in average are factored out
of loop_filter_v_4_8/v_8_8 into a shared filter_core_8() operating purely
on register vectors (samples in the high 8 lanes). The vertical path loads
8 samples per tap by stride; the horizontal path loads 8 rows around the
edge, transposes them into per-tap column vectors, runs the same core, and
transposes the modified taps back before storing. The vertical output is
bit-identical to before the refactor (the removed early-out on an all-zero
flat mask is subsumed by the vec_sel merge, which yields the same result).

transpose_8x8_u8() is a 3-level vec_mergeh/mergel network plus a vec_sld to
un-pack the two-columns-per-vector intermediate; it is an involution so the
same routine serves both the load->core and core->store directions. The
network was verified by exhaustive simulation of the index arithmetic.

All loads use unaligned_load (per-row alignment shift), so no stride%16
assumption is made, matching the fix applied to the vertical filters.

Validated with the qemu -cpu 7400 checkasm harness: vp9dsp.loopfilter real
ALTIVEC dispatch across 7 seeds, the six new h functions confirmed selected
and bit-exact against the C reference; full checkasm suite 529/529 (was 523,
+6 newly-dispatching functions). No non-emulated performance claim is made.

